#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120732} :activity/id #uuid "d014fcfb-88d3-58d4-928d-09436eaced72" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121056 :tutorial/content "There is something interesting about the bases of a vector space, summarized in the theorem below:\n\nIf the vector space $V$ has a basis with $n$ vectors, then any basis of $V$ has exactly $n$ vectors.\n\nThe number $n$ of vectors in any basis of $V$ is called the **dimension of the space** $V$ and is denoted $\\textrm{dim}(V).$\n\nFor example, consider the linear span below:\n![](images/3f/3f4139a1701c74e72ca55b662b1d5c06b02f1d3b6cab9a96b4667439dc196068.svg)\n\nWe know that, in order to find a basis of the span, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $3$rd columns. This tells us that\n$\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $H.$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, *any basis* of $H$ will also have *exactly* two vectors. So, we have $\\textrm{dim}(H)=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6700cc12-77d9-59b2-8ee8-826575be0c12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3517 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f0eb1a4d-9c4f-513f-be21-c3f099b4b267" :step/math-academy-id 19970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119659 :question/id #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5410"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049222}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424595}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "c96304c7-f6d5-5dfd-b33f-9c76165825dd" :step/math-academy-id 19971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119660 :question/id #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" :question/math-academy-id "e-5411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424594}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "aa44487c-2db5-5c3c-bc3c-c62ca070f704" :step/math-academy-id 19972 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121057 :tutorial/content "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n![](images/44/44f00659592bc6ac2742af0105fa493efa93e58e8dd63ee9af56a2d90b0e513a.svg)\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({R}^{0})=0,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{1})=1,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n![](images/c8/c8a606f297f86b56224cb9f50ba68b826e614d4f075b396a4104e19bc77c46e7.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "88c2cca4-9e7b-5ace-b239-798bcab0d26a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3518 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition for Finite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "c692fd19-bc87-5c2c-a172-5d1d8ce64f90" :step/math-academy-id 19974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121069 :tutorial/content "As defined earlier, the dimension of a vector space $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$.\n\nHowever, note that the definition above is only applicable if a vector space has at least one basis with a finite number of elements.\n\n- If a vector space has a basis with a finite number of vectors, then it is called **finite-dimensional**.\n\n- Otherwise, if there is no finite basis, the vector space is called **infinite-dimensional**.\n\nIn linear algebra, we mostly consider finite-dimensional vector spaces." :tutorial/id #uuid "e433abf5-e941-5e63-9642-c3f90b4716a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4226 :tutorial/title "Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "1510c9cb-5dac-5d6c-994f-d10e8a6d47b7" :step/math-academy-id 25615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120733}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120056}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424598} :activity/id #uuid "948a33eb-6d1f-58c5-9917-674dc68468c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3095f8-ebd6-5417-b5fa-228deb53654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345465} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffb8a8a-2e10-5113-8365-30ccd72226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345633} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9aee49-f085-5882-8939-280b36b2183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} {:answer/id #uuid "9125c680-3dee-5b40-9892-fbcf815bf7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346617} {:answer/id #uuid "f560de8e-3259-522f-ba63-0c6f8892c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce3662f-50d2-5467-a302-1a748f1ec224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dbe4152-1f13-5417-b4be-c6eaa0530f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79313" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "0af671dd-0c3a-50f4-a41a-e189faada01e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0077e2b0-9780-54fe-afa4-b670dc9fed69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319393} {:answer/id #uuid "3acae8da-de1f-55f4-a6ed-13701c01de6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319570} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0903cd-0495-574d-b41e-abbe3013ddf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319571} {:answer/id #uuid "87860f14-7358-57f6-a4a5-2a37b1f9c9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319798} {:answer/id #uuid "cf85f8ab-e663-568c-9644-773ac18a9dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319798} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4b9fed-d8b4-5a97-89f5-38bb7b5b308d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2304859f-a364-5093-b7c5-56d02eda6877" :question/math-academy-id "q-35298" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\})=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "166d7282-f4b0-5e00-b772-331ab9716c49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424582} {:answer/id #uuid "5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424583} {:answer/id #uuid "78554db1-b432-51b4-936b-3b8941909583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424585} {:answer/id #uuid "a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9dadc2-a499-5eb3-a904-a3da6ea8a443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd465c3-4977-5d35-b197-a5e9f9656a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34832" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "5ee4ca5d-e09c-5ab8-a217-675407324399" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f09dffb-bf3a-5fbb-b995-ad3a893cd8a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345623} {:answer/id #uuid "4a356632-3dd5-5ea1-a47b-554f60c49d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345987} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc38916-6632-5405-a6d9-6c55765a5bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346748} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ad93bf-d22c-545c-be65-567149ec4f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347352} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7019ea-e533-5abd-9898-ccdde8cd52da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345623} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee16fde-27c3-566c-9103-dab0b178ad0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64e956c1-f323-5e83-8c0c-e6640c910184" :question/math-academy-id "q-79326" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=5$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7637b798-0ec3-5970-8fd6-abfce643c348"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424584} {:answer/id #uuid "93ff253e-bb05-5ca6-8757-db5007d7fe23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424586} {:answer/id #uuid "9b73c0f1-2d21-5d2b-981e-9083bbda05f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424587} {:answer/id #uuid "bc38effb-2332-5570-8872-70a5b22fb92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424589} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590} :answer-field/id #uuid "29131f28-d633-5715-a931-1a4a0aae454c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823" :question/math-academy-id "q-79320" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "8b0be1b0-7fd8-51b2-a3b6-8c5d972e2e97" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424599}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424581}]}
